<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Adaptive-icon background (#289).

    This is NOT a real background layer, and that is deliberate. @mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground is not a foreground in the adaptive-icon sense either: it
    carries the full papercut artwork baked in as an opaque 71.5dp square, centred in the 108dp canvas, filling the 72dp masked viewport almost exactly.
    Under any static mask — circle, squircle, rounded square — the mask cuts into that square and this layer is never seen at all, apart from a ~0.25dp
    hairline at the four edge midpoints where the art falls fractionally short of the viewport.

    So this exists for the only two moments the foreground does not cover: that hairline, and launcher parallax, where the foreground slides against the
    background and exposes a strip of it along the trailing edge.

    A flat colour did that badly. It was #D8F0F0, which matches the top of the artwork and misses the bottom by a mile, because the art is a vertical
    gradient — so parallax produced a hard pale band under the icon. The stops below are measured from the artwork itself, averaged over the outer eighth of
    each side of mipmap-xxxhdpi/ic_launcher_foreground.png so the centred shield does not skew them:

        top #D8F3EC   centre #A3E3D7   bottom #6DD3C5

    Fixing this properly would mean splitting the layers — waves into the background, shield alone on transparency into the foreground. Considered and
    rejected in #289: a real background layer has to fill all 108dp and only 71.5dp of artwork exists, with no vector source anywhere in the repo. A split
    means inventing 18.25dp of wave on every side, at five densities, out of raster, and inpainting the hole the shield's drop shadow leaves behind. The
    icon is the app's identity on Play, so a bad redraw costs every user permanently while the artefact it fixes costs a few, transiently, on the launchers
    that animate the two layers.

    Changing this file cannot regress the common case: the background is only visible where the foreground is not, which under a static mask is nowhere.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">
    <!-- angle 270 runs the gradient top-to-bottom, matching the artwork's own direction. -->
    <gradient
        android:type="linear"
        android:angle="270"
        android:startColor="#D8F3EC"
        android:centerColor="#A3E3D7"
        android:endColor="#6DD3C5" />
</shape>
